<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-finding-the-sum-of-arithmetic-sequences" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Finding the Sum of Arithmetic Sequences</title>
    <!--  -->
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.6 Sequences and Series
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine the sum of terms in arithmetic and geometric series
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Introduce the concept of arithmetic sequences and their sums through a visual and engaging activity.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Ensure learners connect the sum <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \ldots + n </m> to the number of upright small triangles in the large triangle.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity>
        <statement>

            <p>
                Consider a large equilateral triangle divided into smaller congruent equilateral triangles.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Row <m>1</m> has <m>1</m> small triangle</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Row <m>2</m> has <m>2</m> small triangles</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Row <m>3</m> has <m>3</m> small triangles</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Row <m>4</m> has <m>4</m> small triangles</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-triangular-number-activity">
                <caption>A large equilateral triangle divided into 4 rows of smaller triangles.</caption>
                <image width="55%">
                    <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\h}{sqrt(3)/2}
                    % Inverted (downward) triangles — light gray spacers
                    % Between row 0 and row 1: c=0,1,2
                    \foreach \c in {0,1,2} {
                        \filldraw[fill=gray!20, draw=black] ({\c+1},0) -- ({\c+0.5},{\h}) -- ({\c+1.5},{\h}) -- cycle;
                    }
                    % Between row 1 and row 2: c=0,1
                    \foreach \c in {0,1} {
                        \filldraw[fill=gray!20, draw=black] ({\c+1.5},{\h}) -- ({\c+1.0},{2*\h}) -- ({\c+2.0},{2*\h}) -- cycle;
                    }
                    % Between row 2 and top: c=0
                    \filldraw[fill=gray!20, draw=black] (2.0,{2*\h}) -- (1.5,{3*\h}) -- (2.5,{3*\h}) -- cycle;
                    % Row 4 (bottom, r=0): 4 upward triangles — orange (even row)
                    \foreach \c in {0,1,2,3} {
                        \filldraw[fill=orange!70, draw=black] ({\c},0) -- ({\c+1},0) -- ({\c+0.5},{\h}) -- cycle;
                    }
                    % Row 3 (r=1): 3 upward triangles — teal (odd row)
                    \foreach \c in {0,1,2} {
                        \filldraw[fill=teal!60, draw=black] ({\c+0.5},{\h}) -- ({\c+1.5},{\h}) -- ({\c+1.0},{2*\h}) -- cycle;
                    }
                    % Row 2 (r=2): 2 upward triangles — orange (even row)
                    \foreach \c in {0,1} {
                        \filldraw[fill=orange!70, draw=black] ({\c+1.0},{2*\h}) -- ({\c+2.0},{2*\h}) -- ({\c+1.5},{3*\h}) -- cycle;
                    }
                    % Row 1 (top, r=3): 1 upward triangle — teal (odd row)
                    \filldraw[fill=teal!60, draw=black] (1.5,{3*\h}) -- (2.5,{3*\h}) -- (2.0,{4*\h}) -- cycle;
                    % Row labels to the right
                    \node[right] at (4.2,{0.5*\h})  {\small Row 4 \;(4 \(\triangle\)s)};
                    \node[right] at (3.7,{1.5*\h})  {\small Row 3 \;(3 \(\triangle\)s)};
                    \node[right] at (3.2,{2.5*\h})  {\small Row 2 \;(2 \(\triangle\)s)};
                    \node[right] at (2.7,{3.5*\h})  {\small Row 1 \;(1 \(\triangle\))};
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                <term>Work in groups:</term>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Draw a triangle with <m>4</m> rows of small triangles.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Count the number of small triangles in each row.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find the total number of small triangles.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>Record your results:</term>
            </p>
            <tabular top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Row</cell>
                    <cell>Number of Triangles</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>?</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>?</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>?</m></cell>
                </row>

            </tabular>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Extend the pattern to <m>5</m> rows and <m>6</m> rows.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What sequence is formed by the number of triangles in each row?</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What is the common difference?</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Discussion:</term>
            </p>

            <p>
                The number of triangles in each row forms an arithmetic sequence:
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> 1, 2, 3, 4, \dots\amp </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>Challenge:</term>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Find the total number of small triangles in a triangle with <m>10</m> rows.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Is it efficient to keep adding for very large numbers of rows?</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Suggest a faster method.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Generalising:</term>
            </p>

            <p>
                Let the number of rows be <m>n</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                The total number of small triangles is:
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> S_n = \amp\; 1 + 2 + 3 + \dots + n</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Write the sequence forwards and backwards.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Add the two expressions term by term.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What pattern do you observe?</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Hence derive a formula for <m>S_n</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    5 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize the concepts and properties learners discovered in the anchor task.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Show how the formula for the sum of an arithmetic progression generalising the sum <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \ldots n</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
        <p>
            When objects are arranged in rows that increase by a constant number, the pattern forms an arithmetic sequence.
        </p>
        <p>
            In a triangular arrangement, the number of elements in each row increases by <m>1</m>:
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
                <mrow> 1, 2, 3, 4, \dots\amp </mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            The total number of elements is the sum of this arithmetic sequence:
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
                <mrow>S_n = \amp \; 1 + 2 + 3 + \dots + n</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            This can be calculated efficiently using:
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
                <mrow> S_n = \amp \frac{n}{2}(1+n)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Where <m>1</m> is the first term (<m>a</m>), <m>n</m> is the last term (<m>a +(n-1)d </m>), and <m>d</m> is the common difference of the sequence.
        </p>
        <p>
            The general formula for the sum of the first <m>n</m> terms of an arithmetic sequence is:
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
                <mrow> S_n = \amp \frac{n}{2}(2a +(n-1)d)</mrow>
            </md>
            
        </p>
        <p>
            This pattern is known as a triangular number and appears in many real-life arrangements.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->
     <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the following arithmetic sequence, <m>1,4,7,10, ...</m>
            </p>
            <p>
                Find the sum of the first
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>10</m> terms.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>100</m> terms.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>a = 1, d = 3, \;\text{and}\; n = 10 </m>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <m>\therefore S_n = \frac{n}{2}[2a + (n-1)d] </m>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow> S_{10} \amp =\; \frac{10}{2}[2\times 1 + (10-1)3] </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp = \; 5[2+27] </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp = \; 5\times 29 </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp = \; 145 </mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>a = 1, d = 3, \;\text{and}\; n = 100 </m>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <m>\therefore S_n = \frac{n}{2}[2a + (n-1)d] </m>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow> S_{100} \amp =\; \frac{100}{2}[2\times 1 + (100-1)3] </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp = \; 50[2+99 \times 3] </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp = \; 50\times 299 </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp = \; 14{,}950 </mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
     </example>
     <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The sum of the first eight terms of an arithmetic sequence is <m>220</m>. If the third term is <m>17</m>, find the sum of the first six terms.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> S_8 =  \amp\; \frac{8}{2}[2a + (8-1)d] </mrow>
                    <mrow> 220 \amp = \; 4(2a + 7d) </mrow>
                    <mrow> 220 \amp = \; 8a + 28d .......(i) </mrow>
                </md>
                
            </p>
            <p>
                The third term is <m>a + (3-1) d = 17 </m>: <m>17 = a+2d .......(ii)</m>
            </p>
            <p>
                Solving (i) and (ii) simultaneously
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> 17  =\amp \; a+2d </mrow>
                    <mrow> 220 \amp = \; 8a + 28d </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> 220 \amp = \; 8a + 28d </mrow>
                    <mrow> 136 \amp = \; 8a + 16d </mrow>
                    <mrow> \therefore 12d \amp = \; 84 </mrow>
                    <mrow> d \amp = \; 7 </mrow>
                </md>
                
            </p>
            <p>
                Substituting <m>d = 7</m> in equation (ii) gives <m>a = 3</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> \text{Therefore}\; S_6 =  \amp \; \frac{6}{2}[2 \times 3 + (6-1)7] </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \; 3(6 + 35) </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \; 3 \times 41 </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \; 123 </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
     </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->
     <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The sum of the <m>10</m> terms of an arithmetic sequence is <m>400</m>. If the sum of the first <m>6</m> terms is <m>120</m>, find the <m>15^{\text{th}}</m> term.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>975</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The sum of the first <m>20</m> terms of an arithmetic sequence is <m>7\frac{1}{2}</m>. If the third term is <m>2</m>, find the sum of the first <m>13</m> terms.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>2.869</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An arithmetic sequence has common difference <m>-\frac{1}{4}</m> and first term <m>3</m>. Find the number of terms whose sum is zero.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>25</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Two arithmetic sequences have common differences <m>9</m> and <m>3</m> respectively, and first terms <m>2</m> and <m>5</m> respectively. Find the number of terms of each sequence that give a common sum.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>2</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A member of a savings cooperative contributes Ksh <m>2{,}000</m> in the first year and increases her annual contribution by Ksh <m>600</m> each subsequent year.
                    After how many complete years will her total contributions reach at least Ksh <m>37{,}000</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>9</m> complete years.
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Here <m>a = 2000</m>, <m>d = 600</m>, and we need <m>S_n \geq 37{,}000</m>.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>S_n \amp =\; \frac{n}{2}[2(2000) + (n-1)(600)]</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp =\; \frac{n}{2}[4000 + 600n - 600]</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp =\; n(1700 + 300n)</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp =\; 300n^2 + 1700n</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Setting <m>300n^2 + 1700n = 37{,}000</m> gives <m>3n^2 + 17n - 370 = 0</m>.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>n \amp =\; \frac{-17 + \sqrt{17^2 + 4 \times 3 \times 370}}{2 \times 3} = \frac{-17 + \sqrt{4729}}{6} \approx \frac{-17 + 68.8}{6} \approx 8.6</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Since <m>n</m> must be a whole number, she needs <m>9</m> complete years.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
         <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the sequence of tiles shown below:
                <figure xml:id="snake-sequence">
                    <image source="images/snake-sequence.png" width="100%">
                        <shortdescription></shortdescription>
                    </image>
                    <caption><q><url href="https://www.visualpatterns.org/patterns/401-through-450">Pattern 403</url></q> by Johnothon Sauer is licensed under <url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/deed.en">CC-BY-4.0</url>.</caption>
                </figure>
            </p>
            <term>The image shows the 4th, 5th and 6th terms in the sequence, respectively.</term>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>S_n</m> represent the number of tiles in the <m>n^{\text{th}}</m> snake. How many tiles are in the 4th term (the left image), <m>S_4</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The fourth term has <md>S_4 = 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 4 = 20 \text{ tiles}</md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many tiles are in the fifth snake, the middle image, <m>S_5</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The fifth snake has <md>S_5 = S_4 + 5 + 5 = 30 \text{ tiles}</md>, so <m>S_5 = 30</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Can you find a formula for the number of tiles in the <m>n^{\text{th}}</m> snake, <m>S_n</m>? You can write this formula using dots (like <m>\ldots</m>).
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We are adding up natural numbers, each repeated twice, until we reach <m>n</m>. Therefore, the formula for the number of tiles in the <m>n^{\text{th}}</m> snake is:
                    <md>
                        S_n = 2 + 4 + 6 + \ldots + 2n
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Using our formula for the sum of an arithmetic sequence, can you find a single expression for the number of tiles in the <m>n^{\text{th}}</m> snake, <m>S_n</m>, without using dots?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    In our arithmetic series <m>2 + 4 + 6 + \ldots + 2n</m>, the first term is <m>a = 2</m> and the common difference is <m>d = 2</m>. The number of terms is <m>n</m>. Using the formula for the sum of an arithmetic sequence:
                    <md>
                        <mrow> S_n \amp = \; \frac{n}{2}\left[2a + (n-1)d\right] </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \; \frac{n}{2}\left[2\times 2 + (n-1)2\right] </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{n}{2}\left[4 + 2n - 2\right]</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \; n(n+1) </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
     </exercises>

